(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("argocd",{"argocd/pull-based-delivery-and-the-repository-as-source-of-truth":{"html":"\u003cp>In push-based delivery a pipeline holds cluster credentials and runs kubectl or helm against the cluster. In pull-based delivery a controller inside the cluster watches a repository and makes the cluster match what is stored there. The second approach changes who holds credentials, how drift is handled and how you audit changes. Argo CD is the most widely used implementation, and this lesson explains the idea before the tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Contrast push and pull delivery\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain the repository as the source of truth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Describe reconciliation and drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know when this model fits and when it does not\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"push-and-pull\">Push and pull\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Push:\u003c/strong> a CI job builds an image, then runs \u003ccode>kubectl apply\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>helm upgrade\u003c/code> using credentials for the cluster. The job is the only place that knows the result, and credentials must be stored in the CI system. \u003cstrong>Pull:\u003c/strong> CI builds the image and commits a change to a configuration repository (for example, a new image tag). A controller in the cluster notices the commit and applies it. The cluster reaches out; nothing needs inbound access or stored cluster credentials in CI.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-repository-as-source-of-truth\">The repository as source of truth\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The desired state of the cluster is written as manifests, Helm charts or Kustomize overlays in a repository. A change is a commit, reviewed in a pull request, with an author, a message and a history. Rolling back is reverting a commit. The history answers &quot;who changed this, when and why?&quot; without searching through pipeline logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reconciliation-and-drift\">Reconciliation and drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The controller continuously compares \u003cstrong>desired state\u003c/strong> (the repository) with \u003cstrong>live state\u003c/strong> (the cluster). If someone edits a resource by hand, the two diverge: that is \u003cstrong>drift\u003c/strong>. The controller can report it, or put things back. This turns manual hot-fixes from invisible changes into visible differences.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trade-offs\">Trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Benefits: audit trail, consistent environments, simple rollback, fewer credentials in CI, and recovery by pointing a new cluster at the repository. Costs: a separate configuration repository to manage, a delay between commit and rollout, secrets need special handling because the repository must not contain them in plain text, and more concepts for newcomers. It fits Kubernetes well; it fits less well for one-off scripts or resources outside the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Someone edits a Deployment by hand in production. With a pull-based controller using automatic self-healing, what happens?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The controller detects drift and reverts the change to match the repository.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The repository wins.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Pull-based delivery and the repository as source... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Push and pull\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The repository as source of truth\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reconciliation and drift\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Trade-offs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Push and pull to The repository as source of truth; 2, The repository as source of truth to Reconciliation and drift; 3, Reconciliation and drift to Trade-offs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Push and pull \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Push: a CI job builds an image, then runs kubectl apply or helm upgrade using credentials for the cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Mixing application code and cluster configuration without a plan.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>The repository as source of truth \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The desired state of the cluster is written as manifests, Helm charts or Kustomize overlays in a repository.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Treating the repository as optional.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Reconciliation and drift \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The controller continuously compares desired state (the repository) with live state (the cluster).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hand-editing the cluster and expecting it to stay.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Trade-offs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Benefits: audit trail, consistent environments, simple rollback, fewer credentials in CI, and recovery by pointing a new cluster at the repository.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Putting plain-text secrets in it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-hot-fix-that-vanished\">Incident: The hot-fix that vanished\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer scaled a deployment up by hand during a spike, and twenty minutes later it returned to 2 replicas.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl scale deploy/api --replicas=10 -n shop\nkubectl get deploy api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl scale deploy/api --replicas=10 -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME  READY  UP-TO-DATE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api   2/2    2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Automatic self-healing restored the repository value. The team learned to change replicas by commit or by autoscaler, and agreed on an emergency procedure: pause automation for that application, fix, then commit the same change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating the repository as optional\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting plain-text secrets in it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hand-editing the cluster and expecting it to stay\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing application code and cluster configuration without a plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"push-and-pull","text":"Push and pull"},{"id":"the-repository-as-source-of-truth","text":"The repository as source of truth"},{"id":"reconciliation-and-drift","text":"Reconciliation and drift"},{"id":"trade-offs","text":"Trade-offs"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-hot-fix-that-vanished","text":"Incident: The hot-fix that vanished"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD core concepts","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/core_concepts/"},{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"In push-based delivery a pipeline holds cluster credentials and runs kubectl or helm against the cluster. In pull-based delivery a controller inside the cluster watches a repository and makes the cluster match what is stored there. The second approach changes who holds credentials, how drift is handled and how you audit changes. Argo CD is the most widely used implementation, and this lesson explains the idea before the tool. What you will learn Contrast push and pull delivery Explain the repository as the source of truth Describe reconciliation and drift Know when this model fits and when it does not Push and pull Push: a CI job builds an image, then runs kubectl apply or helm upgrade using credentials for the cluster. The job is the only place that knows the result, and credentials must be stored in the CI system. Pull: CI builds the image and commits a change to a configuration repository (for example, a new image tag). A controller in the cluster notices the commit and applies it. The cluster reaches out; nothing needs inbound access or stored cluster credentials in CI. The repository as source of truth The desired state of the cluster is written as manifests, Helm charts or Kustomize overlays in a repository. A change is a commit, reviewed in a pull request, with an author, a message and a history. Rolling back is reverting a commit. The history answers \"who changed this, when and why?\" without searching through pipeline logs. Reconciliation and drift The controller continuously compares desired state (the repository) with live state (the cluster). If someone edits a resource by hand, the two diverge: that is drift . The controller can report it, or put things back. This turns manual hot-fixes from invisible changes into visible differences. Trade-offs Benefits: audit trail, consistent environments, simple rollback, fewer credentials in CI, and recovery by pointing a new cluster at the repository. Costs: a separate configuration repository to manage, a delay between commit and rollout, secrets need special handling because the repository must not contain them in plain text, and more concepts for newcomers. It fits Kubernetes well; it fits less well for one-off scripts or resources outside the cluster. Predict first. Someone edits a Deployment by hand in production. With a pull-based controller using automatic self-healing, what happens? Your guess Answer: The controller detects drift and reverts the change to match the repository. The repository wins. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Pull-based delivery and the repository as source... fits together Flow in order: 1, Push and pull to The repository as source of truth; 2, The repository as source of truth to Reconciliation and drift; 3, Reconciliation and drift to Trade-offs. Step Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Push and pull Step What it does: Push: a CI job builds an image, then runs kubectl apply or helm upgrade using credentials for the cluster. If it fails or is weak: Mixing application code and cluster configuration without a plan. The repository as source of truth Outside party What it does: The desired state of the cluster is written as manifests, Helm charts or Kustomize overlays in a repository. If it fails or is weak: Treating the repository as optional. Reconciliation and drift Step What it does: The controller continuously compares desired state (the repository) with live state (the cluster). If it fails or is weak: Hand-editing the cluster and expecting it to stay. Trade-offs Step What it does: Benefits: audit trail, consistent environments, simple rollback, fewer credentials in CI, and recovery by pointing a new cluster at the repository. If it fails or is weak: Putting plain-text secrets in it. Inciden"},"argocd/installing-argo-cd-and-the-application-resource":{"html":"\u003cp>Argo CD runs in your cluster as a set of components: an API server and UI, a repository server that renders manifests, and an application controller that compares and syncs. You tell it what to deliver with a custom resource called an Application, which names a source in a repository and a destination in a cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Install Argo CD and reach its UI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Name the main components\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write an Application manifest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand source, destination and project\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"install\">Install\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Create a namespace and apply the official install manifest (or use the Helm chart):\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create namespace argocd\nkubectl apply -n argocd -f install.yaml   # the stable install manifest from the Getting started page\nkubectl -n argocd get pods\nkubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath=&quot;{.data.password}&quot; | base64 -d\nkubectl -n argocd port-forward svc/argocd-server 8080:443\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create namespace argocd\nkubectl apply -n argocd -f install.yaml   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># the stable install manifest from the Getting started page\u003c/span>\nkubectl -n argocd get pods\nkubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{.data.password}&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">base64\u003c/span> -d\nkubectl -n argocd port-forward svc/argocd-server 8080:443\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The initial admin password is in a secret; change it or switch to single sign-on and delete the secret. For production, pin a version, run it highly available and put the UI behind TLS and an ingress.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"components\">Components\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>API server\u003c/strong> serves the UI, CLI and API and handles authentication. The \u003cstrong>repo server\u003c/strong> clones repositories and renders manifests from plain YAML, Helm or Kustomize. The \u003cstrong>application controller\u003c/strong> compares the rendered desired state with live state and performs syncs. Redis caches state and optional components handle notifications and ApplicationSets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-application-resource\">The Application resource\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Application\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  project: default\n  source:\n    repoURL: https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\n    targetRevision: main\n    path: apps/shop-api\n  destination:\n    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n    namespace: shop\n  syncPolicy:\n    syncOptions:\n      - CreateNamespace=true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Application\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argocd\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">project:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repoURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetRevision:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">server:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://kubernetes.default.svc\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncPolicy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncOptions:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CreateNamespace=true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>source\u003c/strong> says which repository, revision and folder. The \u003cstrong>destination\u003c/strong> says which cluster and namespace. The \u003cstrong>project\u003c/strong> groups applications and limits what they may do. With no automated sync policy, you press Sync (or run \u003ccode>argocd app sync\u003c/code>) yourself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli-and-ui\">CLI and UI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The CLI logs in with \u003ccode>argocd login\u003c/code>, lists applications with \u003ccode>argocd app list\u003c/code> and shows differences with \u003ccode>argocd app diff shop-api\u003c/code>. The UI displays the resource tree, sync and health status, and events. Both use the same API and the same permissions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You create an Application but nothing appears in the shop namespace. What did you probably forget?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> To sync it: without an automated policy the application stays OutOfSync until someone syncs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Manual by default.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Installing Argo CD and the Application resource fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Components\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The Application resource\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CLI and UI\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Install to Components; 2, Components to The Application resource; 3, The Application resource to CLI and UI.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Install \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Create a namespace and apply the official install manifest (or use the Helm chart).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Leaving the initial admin password in place.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Components \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The API server serves the UI, CLI and API and handles authentication.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Running the default project with no limits.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>The Application resource \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The source says which repository, revision and folder.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using a write-capable credential to read a repository.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>CLI and UI \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The CLI logs in with argocd login, lists applications with argocd app list and shows differences with argocd app diff shop-api.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Installing without pinning a version.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-unknown-repository\">Incident: Unknown repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new application showed &quot;ComparisonError: repository not accessible&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app get shop-api | head -8\nargocd repo list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app get shop-api | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Sync Status: Unknown\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Condition: ComparisonError: rpc error: authentication required\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd repo list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">TYPE  NAME  REPO  STATUS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(none)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The repository was private and had not been registered. The team added it with a read-only deploy credential through the repository settings, and the application compared successfully. They kept credentials in the cluster as secrets and not in the Application manifest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaving the initial admin password in place\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running the default project with no limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a write-capable credential to read a repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing without pinning a version\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"install","text":"Install"},{"id":"components","text":"Components"},{"id":"the-application-resource","text":"The Application resource"},{"id":"cli-and-ui","text":"CLI and UI"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-unknown-repository","text":"Incident: Unknown repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD getting started","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/getting_started/"},{"t":"Argo CD core concepts","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/core_concepts/"}],"text":"Argo CD runs in your cluster as a set of components: an API server and UI, a repository server that renders manifests, and an application controller that compares and syncs. You tell it what to deliver with a custom resource called an Application, which names a source in a repository and a destination in a cluster. What you will learn Install Argo CD and reach its UI Name the main components Write an Application manifest Understand source, destination and project Install Create a namespace and apply the official install manifest (or use the Helm chart): kubectl create namespace argocd kubectl apply -n argocd -f install.yaml # the stable install manifest from the Getting started page kubectl -n argocd get pods kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath= \"{.data.password}\" | base64 -d kubectl -n argocd port-forward svc/argocd-server 8080:443 The initial admin password is in a secret; change it or switch to single sign-on and delete the secret. For production, pin a version, run it highly available and put the UI behind TLS and an ingress. Components The API server serves the UI, CLI and API and handles authentication. The repo server clones repositories and renders manifests from plain YAML, Helm or Kustomize. The application controller compares the rendered desired state with live state and performs syncs. Redis caches state and optional components handle notifications and ApplicationSets. The Application resource apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Application metadata: name: shop-api namespace: argocd spec: project: default source: repoURL: https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git targetRevision: main path: apps/shop-api destination: server: https://kubernetes.default.svc namespace: shop syncPolicy: syncOptions: - CreateNamespace=true The source says which repository, revision and folder. The destination says which cluster and namespace. The project groups applications and limits what they may do. With no automated sync policy, you press Sync (or run argocd app sync ) yourself. CLI and UI The CLI logs in with argocd login , lists applications with argocd app list and shows differences with argocd app diff shop-api . The UI displays the resource tree, sync and health status, and events. Both use the same API and the same permissions. Predict first. You create an Application but nothing appears in the shop namespace. What did you probably forget? Your guess Answer: To sync it: without an automated policy the application stays OutOfSync until someone syncs. Manual by default. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Installing Argo CD and the Application resource fits together Flow in order: 1, Install to Components; 2, Components to The Application resource; 3, The Application resource to CLI and UI. Step Client Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Install Step What it does: Create a namespace and apply the official install manifest (or use the Helm chart). If it fails or is weak: Leaving the initial admin password in place. Components Step What it does: The API server serves the UI, CLI and API and handles authentication. If it fails or is weak: Running the default project with no limits. The Application resource Step What it does: The source says which repository, revision and folder. If it fails or is weak: Using a write-capable credential to read a repository. CLI and UI Client What it does: The CLI logs in with argocd login, lists applications with argocd app list and shows differences with argocd app diff shop-api. If it fails or is weak: Installing without pinning a version. Incident: Unknown repository Incident A new application showed \"ComparisonError: repository not accessible\". $ argocd app get shop-api | head -8 Sync Status: Unknown Condition: Comparison"},"argocd/sync-health-and-drift":{"html":"\u003cp>Argo CD shows two separate statuses for every application. Sync status says whether live state matches the repository. Health status says whether the resources actually work. They are independent, and reading them together tells you what to do next. Automation options such as prune and self-heal are powerful and need care.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Read Synced, OutOfSync, Healthy, Progressing and Degraded\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a sync policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand prune and self-heal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use sync options and ignore rules for expected differences\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-statuses\">Two statuses\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Sync status:\u003c/strong> Synced (live matches desired), OutOfSync (they differ) or Unknown (cannot compare). \u003cstrong>Health:\u003c/strong> Healthy, Progressing (rolling out), Degraded (failing), Suspended, Missing. An application can be Synced but Degraded: the right manifests are applied but the pods crash. It can be OutOfSync but Healthy: someone changed a replica count and the app still works.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"manual-or-automated\">Manual or automated\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With manual sync, Argo CD shows differences and waits for a person or pipeline to sync. With \u003cstrong>automated\u003c/strong> sync, it applies new commits on its own. Two further switches matter. \u003cstrong>Prune\u003c/strong> deletes resources that are no longer in the repository. \u003cstrong>Self-heal\u003c/strong> reverts live changes made outside the repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"syncPolicy:\n  automated:\n    prune: true\n    selfHeal: true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncPolicy:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">automated:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prune:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selfHeal:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use automation for non-production first. For production, many teams automate but keep prune off until they trust their repository layout, or require a manual sync for risky applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-prune-needs-care\">Why prune needs care\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Prune removes anything Argo CD manages that disappears from the source. A renamed folder, a wrong path or an empty render can look like &quot;delete everything&quot;. Protect critical resources with the annotation \u003ccode>argocd.argoproj.io/sync-options: Prune=false\u003c/code>, and use sync windows or manual approval for sensitive applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expected-differences\">Expected differences\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Controllers add fields, such as an autoscaler changing replicas or a webhook injecting certificates. Tell Argo CD to ignore them with \u003ccode>ignoreDifferences\u003c/code> for those paths, or the application will show OutOfSync forever. Sync options like \u003ccode>ServerSideApply=true\u003c/code> help when several controllers manage one object.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retrying\">Retrying\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A sync can fail on a bad manifest. Use \u003ccode>retry\u003c/code> settings for transient problems, and read the sync result message for permanent ones. Argo CD records sync history so you can see what was applied at each revision.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An app is Synced but Degraded. What does that tell you?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The manifests match the repository, but the workload is failing; look at pod events and logs, not at the diff.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Separate questions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Sync, health and drift fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Two statuses\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Manual or automated\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Why prune needs care\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Expected differences\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retrying\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Two statuses to Manual or automated; 2, Manual or automated to Why prune needs care; 3, Why prune needs care to Expected differences; 4, Expected differences to Retrying.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Two statuses \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sync status: Synced (live matches desired), OutOfSync (they differ) or Unknown (cannot compare).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reading only sync status and ignoring health.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Manual or automated \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> With manual sync, Argo CD shows differences and waits for a person or pipeline to sync.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Turning on prune everywhere on day one.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Why prune needs care \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prune removes anything Argo CD manages that disappears from the source.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Expected differences \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Controllers add fields, such as an autoscaler changing replicas or a webhook injecting certificates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fighting controllers instead of ignoring fields.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Retrying \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A sync can fail on a bad manifest. Use retry settings for transient problems, and read the sync result message for permanent ones.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No review for repository restructures.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-prune-that-removed-a-namespace\">Incident: The prune that removed a namespace\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a folder rename, an automated application with prune turned on deleted its resources.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app get shop-api --refresh | head -6\nargocd app diff shop-api | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app get shop-api --refresh | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Sync Status: OutOfSync\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Sync Policy: Automated (Prune)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app diff shop-api | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">===== apps/Deployment shop/api ======\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt; (deleted in source)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The path in the Application no longer matched the folder, so the render was empty and prune removed everything. The team restored the path, resynced, then set Prune=false on stateful resources and added a pre-merge check that renders the manifests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Turning on prune everywhere on day one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading only sync status and ignoring health\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fighting controllers instead of ignoring fields\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No review for repository restructures\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-statuses","text":"Two statuses"},{"id":"manual-or-automated","text":"Manual or automated"},{"id":"why-prune-needs-care","text":"Why prune needs care"},{"id":"expected-differences","text":"Expected differences"},{"id":"retrying","text":"Retrying"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-prune-that-removed-a-namespace","text":"Incident: The prune that removed a namespace"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Automated sync policy","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/auto_sync/"},{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"Argo CD shows two separate statuses for every application. Sync status says whether live state matches the repository. Health status says whether the resources actually work. They are independent, and reading them together tells you what to do next. Automation options such as prune and self-heal are powerful and need care. What you will learn Read Synced, OutOfSync, Healthy, Progressing and Degraded Choose a sync policy Understand prune and self-heal Use sync options and ignore rules for expected differences Two statuses Sync status: Synced (live matches desired), OutOfSync (they differ) or Unknown (cannot compare). Health: Healthy, Progressing (rolling out), Degraded (failing), Suspended, Missing. An application can be Synced but Degraded: the right manifests are applied but the pods crash. It can be OutOfSync but Healthy: someone changed a replica count and the app still works. Manual or automated With manual sync, Argo CD shows differences and waits for a person or pipeline to sync. With automated sync, it applies new commits on its own. Two further switches matter. Prune deletes resources that are no longer in the repository. Self-heal reverts live changes made outside the repository. syncPolicy: automated: prune: true selfHeal: true Use automation for non-production first. For production, many teams automate but keep prune off until they trust their repository layout, or require a manual sync for risky applications. Why prune needs care Prune removes anything Argo CD manages that disappears from the source. A renamed folder, a wrong path or an empty render can look like \"delete everything\". Protect critical resources with the annotation argocd.argoproj.io/sync-options: Prune=false , and use sync windows or manual approval for sensitive applications. Expected differences Controllers add fields, such as an autoscaler changing replicas or a webhook injecting certificates. Tell Argo CD to ignore them with ignoreDifferences for those paths, or the application will show OutOfSync forever. Sync options like ServerSideApply=true help when several controllers manage one object. Retrying A sync can fail on a bad manifest. Use retry settings for transient problems, and read the sync result message for permanent ones. Argo CD records sync history so you can see what was applied at each revision. Predict first. An app is Synced but Degraded. What does that tell you? Your guess Answer: The manifests match the repository, but the workload is failing; look at pod events and logs, not at the diff. Separate questions. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Sync, health and drift fits together Flow in order: 1, Two statuses to Manual or automated; 2, Manual or automated to Why prune needs care; 3, Why prune needs care to Expected differences; 4, Expected differences to Retrying. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Two statuses Step What it does: Sync status: Synced (live matches desired), OutOfSync (they differ) or Unknown (cannot compare). If it fails or is weak: Reading only sync status and ignoring health. Manual or automated Step What it does: With manual sync, Argo CD shows differences and waits for a person or pipeline to sync. If it fails or is weak: Turning on prune everywhere on day one. Why prune needs care Step What it does: Prune removes anything Argo CD manages that disappears from the source. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Expected differences Step What it does: Controllers add fields, such as an autoscaler changing replicas or a webhook injecting certificates. If it fails or is weak: Fighting controllers instead of ignoring fields. Retrying Step What it does: A sync can fail on a bad manifest. Use retry settings "},"argocd/helm-and-kustomize-with-argo-cd":{"html":"\u003cp>Most real repositories use Helm charts, Kustomize overlays or both. Argo CD renders them itself and applies the result as plain manifests. This has consequences: Helm hooks and release records work differently, and values must live in the repository to stay reviewable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Point an Application at a Helm chart or a Kustomize folder\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set values per environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know how Argo CD treats Helm releases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pin chart versions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"helm-sources\">Helm sources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The Application source can be a chart in a Helm repository or a folder in the repository. Values come from a values file or inline parameters.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"source:\n  repoURL: https://charts.example.com\n  chart: shop-api\n  targetRevision: 1.4.2\n  helm:\n    valueFiles:\n      - values-prod.yaml\n    parameters:\n      - name: replicaCount\n        value: &quot;4&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repoURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://charts.example.com\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">chart:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetRevision:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.2\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">helm:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">valueFiles:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">parameters:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">replicaCount\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;4&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Argo CD runs the equivalent of \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> and applies the output. It does not run \u003ccode>helm install\u003c/code>, so \u003ccode>helm list\u003c/code> will not show the release and Helm release history is not used. Argo CD tracks history itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kustomize\">Kustomize\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Point at a folder with a \u003ccode>kustomization.yaml\u003c/code>. An overlay per environment patches a shared base: change replicas, resource limits, image tags and config. \u003ccode>kustomize edit set image\u003c/code> or the Application&#39;s \u003ccode>kustomize.images\u003c/code> field updates the image.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-values-in-the-repository\">Keep values in the repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Inline overrides set in the UI become live state that nobody reviewed. Prefer values files committed to the repository, one per environment, so a diff in a pull request shows exactly what changes in production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pin-versions\">Pin versions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use an exact chart version, not a floating range or \u003ccode>HEAD\u003c/code>. Update by a pull request that shows the rendered difference. Tools that render manifests in the pull request make review easy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hooks-and-tests\">Hooks and tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Helm hooks are converted into Argo CD hooks where possible, but behaviour differs from Helm&#39;s release lifecycle. Test the render locally with \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>kustomize build\u003c/code> and compare with \u003ccode>argocd app diff\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Why does helm list not show a release deployed by Argo CD?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Argo CD renders the chart and applies the manifests itself, without creating a Helm release.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Different tracking.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Helm and Kustomize with Argo CD fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Helm sources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kustomize\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Keep values in the repository\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pin versions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hooks and tests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Helm sources to Kustomize; 2, Kustomize to Keep values in the repository; 3, Keep values in the repository to Pin versions; 4, Pin versions to Hooks and tests.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Helm sources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The Application source can be a chart in a Helm repository or a folder in the repository.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assuming Helm release commands work.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Kustomize \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Point at a folder with a kustomization.yaml.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Keep values in the repository \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Inline overrides set in the UI become live state that nobody reviewed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overrides outside the repository.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Pin versions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Use an exact chart version, not a floating range or HEAD.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Floating chart versions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Hooks and tests \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Helm hooks are converted into Argo CD hooks where possible, but behaviour differs from Helm's release lifecycle.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not rendering locally before merge.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-value-nobody-reviewed\">Incident: The value nobody reviewed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A parameter was changed in the UI to hotfix replicas, and a later commit silently reverted it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app get shop-api -o yaml | grep -A3 parameters\ngrep replicaCount apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app get shop-api -o yaml | grep -A3 parameters\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    parameters:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    - name: replicaCount\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      value: &quot;8&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep replicaCount apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">replicaCount: 4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>UI parameters override files, so the hotfix existed only in the application object. The team moved the value into the file, removed the override, and made the UI read-only for parameters on production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Overrides outside the repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Floating chart versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming Helm release commands work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not rendering locally before merge\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"helm-sources","text":"Helm sources"},{"id":"kustomize","text":"Kustomize"},{"id":"keep-values-in-the-repository","text":"Keep values in the repository"},{"id":"pin-versions","text":"Pin versions"},{"id":"hooks-and-tests","text":"Hooks and tests"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-value-nobody-reviewed","text":"Incident: The value nobody reviewed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm in Argo CD","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/helm/"},{"t":"Kustomize in Argo CD","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/kustomize/"}],"text":"Most real repositories use Helm charts, Kustomize overlays or both. Argo CD renders them itself and applies the result as plain manifests. This has consequences: Helm hooks and release records work differently, and values must live in the repository to stay reviewable. What you will learn Point an Application at a Helm chart or a Kustomize folder Set values per environment Know how Argo CD treats Helm releases Pin chart versions Helm sources The Application source can be a chart in a Helm repository or a folder in the repository. Values come from a values file or inline parameters. source: repoURL: https://charts.example.com chart: shop-api targetRevision: 1.4 .2 helm: valueFiles: - values-prod.yaml parameters: - name: replicaCount value: \"4\" Argo CD runs the equivalent of helm template and applies the output. It does not run helm install , so helm list will not show the release and Helm release history is not used. Argo CD tracks history itself. Kustomize Point at a folder with a kustomization.yaml . An overlay per environment patches a shared base: change replicas, resource limits, image tags and config. kustomize edit set image or the Application&#39;s kustomize.images field updates the image. Keep values in the repository Inline overrides set in the UI become live state that nobody reviewed. Prefer values files committed to the repository, one per environment, so a diff in a pull request shows exactly what changes in production. Pin versions Use an exact chart version, not a floating range or HEAD . Update by a pull request that shows the rendered difference. Tools that render manifests in the pull request make review easy. Hooks and tests Helm hooks are converted into Argo CD hooks where possible, but behaviour differs from Helm&#39;s release lifecycle. Test the render locally with helm template or kustomize build and compare with argocd app diff . Predict first. Why does helm list not show a release deployed by Argo CD? Your guess Answer: Argo CD renders the chart and applies the manifests itself, without creating a Helm release. Different tracking. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Helm and Kustomize with Argo CD fits together Flow in order: 1, Helm sources to Kustomize; 2, Kustomize to Keep values in the repository; 3, Keep values in the repository to Pin versions; 4, Pin versions to Hooks and tests. Step Outside party Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Helm sources Step What it does: The Application source can be a chart in a Helm repository or a folder in the repository. If it fails or is weak: Assuming Helm release commands work. Kustomize Step What it does: Point at a folder with a kustomization.yaml. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Keep values in the repository Outside party What it does: Inline overrides set in the UI become live state that nobody reviewed. If it fails or is weak: Overrides outside the repository. Pin versions Step What it does: Use an exact chart version, not a floating range or HEAD. If it fails or is weak: Floating chart versions. Hooks and tests Observability What it does: Helm hooks are converted into Argo CD hooks where possible, but behaviour differs from Helm's release lifecycle. If it fails or is weak: Not rendering locally before merge. Incident: The value nobody reviewed Incident A parameter was changed in the UI to hotfix replicas, and a later commit silently reverted it. $ argocd app get shop-api -o yaml | grep -A3 parameters parameters: - name: replicaCount value: \"8\" $ grep replicaCount apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml replicaCount: 4 UI parameters override files, so the hotfix existed only in the application object. The team moved the value into the file, "},"argocd/environments-app-of-apps-and-applicationsets":{"html":"\u003cp>One application is easy. Fifty applications across dev, staging and production, and several clusters, need structure. The goal is that adding an application or an environment is a small, reviewable change, and promotion between environments is a commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose a repository layout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use the app-of-apps pattern\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Generate applications with ApplicationSets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Promote changes between environments\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layout\">Layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A common layout has a folder per application with a base and overlays per environment, or a folder per environment that points at shared charts. Keep application source code and configuration in separate repositories when possible, so a new image tag is a small commit that does not trigger application tests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"app-of-apps\">App-of-apps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A parent Application points at a folder full of Application manifests. Syncing the parent creates all the children. This lets you bootstrap a whole cluster from one command, and add a new application by adding one file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"applicationsets\">ApplicationSets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>ApplicationSet\u003c/strong> generates Applications from a template and a generator. A list generator makes one per environment; a cluster generator makes one per registered cluster; a directory generator makes one per folder in the repository; a pull request generator can create temporary preview environments.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ApplicationSet\nmetadata:\n  name: shop\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  generators:\n    - list:\n        elements:\n          - env: staging\n          - env: production\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      name: &quot;shop-{{env}}&quot;\n    spec:\n      project: default\n      source:\n        repoURL: https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\n        targetRevision: main\n        path: &quot;overlays/{{env}}&quot;\n      destination:\n        server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n        namespace: &quot;shop-{{env}}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ApplicationSet\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argocd\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">generators:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">list:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">elements:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">staging\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{env}}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">project:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repoURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetRevision:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;overlays/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{env}}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">server:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://kubernetes.default.svc\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{env}}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"promotion\">Promotion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Promote by copying a tested image tag or chart version from the staging overlay to production in a pull request, ideally automated after checks pass. Avoid promoting by branch merges that carry unrelated changes. Production should require review and a clear record.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blast-radius\">Blast radius\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A generator that is mistaken can create or remove many applications at once. Use projects, review for ApplicationSet changes, and policies that limit deletion (for example, preserving resources when an application is removed).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You want one Application per cluster registered in Argo CD. Which feature fits?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> An ApplicationSet with a cluster generator.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Generated applications.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Environments, app-of-apps and ApplicationSets fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Layout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">App-of-apps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">ApplicationSets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Promotion\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Blast radius\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Layout to App-of-apps; 2, App-of-apps to ApplicationSets; 3, ApplicationSets to Promotion; 4, Promotion to Blast radius.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Layout \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A common layout has a folder per application with a base and overlays per environment, or a folder per environment that points at shared charts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One giant repository folder with no structure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>App-of-apps \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A parent Application points at a folder full of Application manifests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Copy-pasted Application files.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>ApplicationSets \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An ApplicationSet generates Applications from a template and a generator.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No review on generators.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Promotion \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Promote by copying a tested image tag or chart version from the staging overlay to production in a pull request, ideally automated after checks pass.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Promoting with merges that carry other changes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Blast radius \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A generator that is mistaken can create or remove many applications at once.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-fifty-applications-gone\">Incident: Fifty applications gone\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change to an ApplicationSet filter matched nothing and the controller started removing the generated applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd appset get shop | head -4\nargocd app list | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd appset get shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Name: shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Generators: git directories: apps/*\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app list | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A folder rename left the generator with no matches. The team restored the path, set the policy that keeps generated applications when the set changes, and required a second reviewer on ApplicationSet files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One giant repository folder with no structure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copy-pasted Application files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Promoting with merges that carry other changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No review on generators\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layout","text":"Layout"},{"id":"app-of-apps","text":"App-of-apps"},{"id":"applicationsets","text":"ApplicationSets"},{"id":"promotion","text":"Promotion"},{"id":"blast-radius","text":"Blast radius"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-fifty-applications-gone","text":"Incident: Fifty applications gone"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ApplicationSet controller","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/applicationset/"},{"t":"Cluster bootstrapping","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/cluster-bootstrapping/"}],"text":"One application is easy. Fifty applications across dev, staging and production, and several clusters, need structure. The goal is that adding an application or an environment is a small, reviewable change, and promotion between environments is a commit. What you will learn Choose a repository layout Use the app-of-apps pattern Generate applications with ApplicationSets Promote changes between environments Layout A common layout has a folder per application with a base and overlays per environment, or a folder per environment that points at shared charts. Keep application source code and configuration in separate repositories when possible, so a new image tag is a small commit that does not trigger application tests. App-of-apps A parent Application points at a folder full of Application manifests. Syncing the parent creates all the children. This lets you bootstrap a whole cluster from one command, and add a new application by adding one file. ApplicationSets An ApplicationSet generates Applications from a template and a generator. A list generator makes one per environment; a cluster generator makes one per registered cluster; a directory generator makes one per folder in the repository; a pull request generator can create temporary preview environments. apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: ApplicationSet metadata: name: shop namespace: argocd spec: generators: - list: elements: - env: staging - env: production template: metadata: name: \"shop- {{env}} \" spec: project: default source: repoURL: https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git targetRevision: main path: \"overlays/ {{env}} \" destination: server: https://kubernetes.default.svc namespace: \"shop- {{env}} \" Promotion Promote by copying a tested image tag or chart version from the staging overlay to production in a pull request, ideally automated after checks pass. Avoid promoting by branch merges that carry unrelated changes. Production should require review and a clear record. Blast radius A generator that is mistaken can create or remove many applications at once. Use projects, review for ApplicationSet changes, and policies that limit deletion (for example, preserving resources when an application is removed). Predict first. You want one Application per cluster registered in Argo CD. Which feature fits? Your guess Answer: An ApplicationSet with a cluster generator. Generated applications. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Environments, app-of-apps and ApplicationSets fits together Flow in order: 1, Layout to App-of-apps; 2, App-of-apps to ApplicationSets; 3, ApplicationSets to Promotion; 4, Promotion to Blast radius. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Layout Step What it does: A common layout has a folder per application with a base and overlays per environment, or a folder per environment that points at shared charts. If it fails or is weak: One giant repository folder with no structure. App-of-apps Step What it does: A parent Application points at a folder full of Application manifests. If it fails or is weak: Copy-pasted Application files. ApplicationSets Step What it does: An ApplicationSet generates Applications from a template and a generator. If it fails or is weak: No review on generators. Promotion Step What it does: Promote by copying a tested image tag or chart version from the staging overlay to production in a pull request, ideally automated after checks pass. If it fails or is weak: Promoting with merges that carry other changes. Blast radius Step What it does: A generator that is mistaken can create or remove many applications at once. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Incident: Fifty applications gone Incident A change to an Applicati"},"argocd/hooks-sync-waves-and-progressive-delivery":{"html":"\u003cp>Real releases have order: create the namespace and the database schema before the application, run a migration job before pods start, smoke-test afterwards. Argo CD provides hooks and sync waves for this, and a companion project, Argo Rollouts, adds canary and blue-green rollouts with automated analysis.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Order resources with sync waves\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run jobs with hooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Describe canary and blue-green strategies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain how analysis can stop a bad rollout\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sync-waves\">Sync waves\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Annotate resources with \u003ccode>argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: &quot;N&quot;\u003c/code>. Lower numbers sync first, and Argo CD waits for each wave to be healthy before starting the next. Default is wave 0. Use negative waves for namespaces and CRDs, and higher ones for things that depend on the application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hooks\">Hooks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A hook is a resource, usually a Job, annotated with \u003ccode>argocd.argoproj.io/hook\u003c/code>. \u003cstrong>PreSync\u003c/strong> runs before the main sync (database migration), \u003cstrong>Sync\u003c/strong> during it, \u003cstrong>PostSync\u003c/strong> afterwards (smoke test), \u003cstrong>SyncFail\u003c/strong> when it fails. Set \u003ccode>hook-delete-policy\u003c/code> such as \u003ccode>HookSucceeded\u003c/code> so completed jobs do not pile up. Make hooks idempotent, because syncs can be retried.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"canary-and-blue-green\">Canary and blue-green\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A plain Deployment does a rolling update with no view of user impact. \u003cstrong>Argo Rollouts\u003c/strong> replaces it with a Rollout resource. A \u003cstrong>canary\u003c/strong> sends a small share of traffic (5 percent, then 25, 50, 100) to the new version with pauses. \u003cstrong>Blue-green\u003c/strong> runs the new version beside the old and switches traffic at once after checks, keeping the old for fast rollback. Traffic splitting uses an ingress controller or a service mesh.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"automated-analysis\">Automated analysis\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An AnalysisTemplate queries metrics, for example the error rate in Prometheus. If the new version breaks the threshold during a canary step, the rollout aborts and traffic returns to the stable version without a person. This ties delivery to your service level objectives.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-it-simple-first\">Keep it simple first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with sync waves and a PreSync migration. Add canary only when you have good metrics and enough traffic to see a signal in minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A migration Job must run before the new pods start. Which Argo CD feature fits?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A PreSync hook on the Job.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Hooks run around the sync.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Hooks, sync waves and progressive delivery fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sync waves\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hooks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Canary and blue-green\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Automated analysis\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Keep it simple first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Sync waves to Hooks; 2, Hooks to Canary and blue-green; 3, Canary and blue-green to Automated analysis; 4, Automated analysis to Keep it simple first.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Sync waves \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Annotate resources with argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: &quot;N&quot;.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Waves that deadlock on health.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Hooks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A hook is a resource, usually a Job, annotated with argocd.argoproj.io/hook.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Non-idempotent hooks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Canary and blue-green \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A plain Deployment does a rolling update with no view of user impact.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Canary without metrics.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Automated analysis \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An AnalysisTemplate queries metrics, for example the error rate in Prometheus.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the new version breaks the threshold during a canary step, the rollout aborts and traffic returns to the stable version without a person.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Keep it simple first \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start with sync waves and a PreSync migration.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-migration-that-ran-twice\">Incident: The migration that ran twice\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A retried sync ran a PreSync migration a second time and failed on a duplicate column.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs job/migrate -n shop\nkubectl get job migrate -n shop -o yaml | grep hook\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs job/migrate -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR: column &quot;currency&quot; of relation &quot;orders&quot; already exists\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get job migrate -n shop -o yaml | grep hook\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">argocd.argoproj.io/hook: PreSync\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The migration was not idempotent. The team rewrote it to check for the column first, recorded versions in a migrations table, and set the hook delete policy so each run starts clean.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Non-idempotent hooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Waves that deadlock on health\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canary without metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No rollback plan for data changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"sync-waves","text":"Sync waves"},{"id":"hooks","text":"Hooks"},{"id":"canary-and-blue-green","text":"Canary and blue-green"},{"id":"automated-analysis","text":"Automated analysis"},{"id":"keep-it-simple-first","text":"Keep it simple first"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-migration-that-ran-twice","text":"Incident: The migration that ran twice"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Sync phases and waves","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/sync-waves/"},{"t":"Resource hooks","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/resource_hooks/"}],"text":"Real releases have order: create the namespace and the database schema before the application, run a migration job before pods start, smoke-test afterwards. Argo CD provides hooks and sync waves for this, and a companion project, Argo Rollouts, adds canary and blue-green rollouts with automated analysis. What you will learn Order resources with sync waves Run jobs with hooks Describe canary and blue-green strategies Explain how analysis can stop a bad rollout Sync waves Annotate resources with argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: \"N\" . Lower numbers sync first, and Argo CD waits for each wave to be healthy before starting the next. Default is wave 0. Use negative waves for namespaces and CRDs, and higher ones for things that depend on the application. Hooks A hook is a resource, usually a Job, annotated with argocd.argoproj.io/hook . PreSync runs before the main sync (database migration), Sync during it, PostSync afterwards (smoke test), SyncFail when it fails. Set hook-delete-policy such as HookSucceeded so completed jobs do not pile up. Make hooks idempotent, because syncs can be retried. Canary and blue-green A plain Deployment does a rolling update with no view of user impact. Argo Rollouts replaces it with a Rollout resource. A canary sends a small share of traffic (5 percent, then 25, 50, 100) to the new version with pauses. Blue-green runs the new version beside the old and switches traffic at once after checks, keeping the old for fast rollback. Traffic splitting uses an ingress controller or a service mesh. Automated analysis An AnalysisTemplate queries metrics, for example the error rate in Prometheus. If the new version breaks the threshold during a canary step, the rollout aborts and traffic returns to the stable version without a person. This ties delivery to your service level objectives. Keep it simple first Start with sync waves and a PreSync migration. Add canary only when you have good metrics and enough traffic to see a signal in minutes. Predict first. A migration Job must run before the new pods start. Which Argo CD feature fits? Your guess Answer: A PreSync hook on the Job. Hooks run around the sync. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Hooks, sync waves and progressive delivery fits together Flow in order: 1, Sync waves to Hooks; 2, Hooks to Canary and blue-green; 3, Canary and blue-green to Automated analysis; 4, Automated analysis to Keep it simple first. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Sync waves Step What it does: Annotate resources with argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: \"N\". If it fails or is weak: Waves that deadlock on health. Hooks Step What it does: A hook is a resource, usually a Job, annotated with argocd.argoproj.io/hook. If it fails or is weak: Non-idempotent hooks. Canary and blue-green Step What it does: A plain Deployment does a rolling update with no view of user impact. If it fails or is weak: Canary without metrics. Automated analysis Step What it does: An AnalysisTemplate queries metrics, for example the error rate in Prometheus. If it fails or is weak: If the new version breaks the threshold during a canary step, the rollout aborts and traffic returns to the stable version without a person. Keep it simple first Step What it does: Start with sync waves and a PreSync migration. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Incident: The migration that ran twice Incident A retried sync ran a PreSync migration a second time and failed on a duplicate column. $ kubectl logs job/migrate -n shop ERROR: column \"currency\" of relation \"orders\" already exists $ kubectl get job migrate -n shop -o yaml | grep hook argocd.argoproj.io/hook: PreSync The migration was not idempotent. The team rewrote it to c"},"argocd/access-projects-secrets-and-multi-cluster":{"html":"\u003cp>Argo CD is powerful: it can create anything in the clusters it manages, so protecting it matters. Three topics decide whether a shared installation is safe: who may do what (projects, RBAC and single sign-on), where secrets live, and how remote clusters are registered.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use projects to limit sources, destinations and kinds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Configure RBAC and single sign-on\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handle secrets without storing them in plain text\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Register and secure multiple clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"projects\">Projects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>AppProject\u003c/strong> limits which repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to, and which resource kinds it may create (for example, no cluster roles). Give each team a project. Do not run everything in \u003ccode>default\u003c/code>, which is unrestricted.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: AppProject\nmetadata:\n  name: team-shop\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  sourceRepos: [&quot;https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git&quot;]\n  destinations:\n    - server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n      namespace: &quot;shop-*&quot;\n  clusterResourceWhitelist: []\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AppProject\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">team-shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argocd\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">sourceRepos:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destinations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">server:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://kubernetes.default.svc\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-*&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">clusterResourceWhitelist:\u003c/span> []\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rbac-and-sign-in\">RBAC and sign-in\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Connect single sign-on (OpenID Connect) so people use company identities and MFA, and disable the shared admin account. Map groups to roles in \u003ccode>argocd-rbac-cm\u003c/code>: viewers see, developers sync their project, platform administrators manage projects. Use project roles and tokens for automation with limited scope.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets\">Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The repository must not hold plain secrets. Common approaches: \u003cstrong>sealed or encrypted secrets\u003c/strong> committed in encrypted form, \u003cstrong>External Secrets Operator\u003c/strong> that creates Kubernetes Secrets from a cloud secrets manager, or a \u003cstrong>plugin\u003c/strong> that injects values at render time. Choose one and standardise it. Remember that Argo CD itself stores cluster credentials and repository credentials as secrets, so protect its namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"multiple-clusters\">Multiple clusters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Register a remote cluster with \u003ccode>argocd cluster add\u003c/code>, which creates a service account in that cluster and stores its token in Argo CD. Prefer a hub model with one control cluster and narrow roles per target, or one Argo CD per environment tier so a compromise of non-production cannot reach production. Limit what the remote service account can do where possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Teams share one Argo CD. How do you stop team A deploying into team B's namespaces?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Give each team an AppProject with its own allowed destinations and sources, and bind roles to that project.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Projects are the boundary.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Access, projects, secrets and multi-cluster fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Projects\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">RBAC and sign-in\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Secrets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Multiple clusters\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Projects to RBAC and sign-in; 2, RBAC and sign-in to Secrets; 3, Secrets to Multiple clusters.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Projects \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An AppProject limits which repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to, and which resource kinds it may create (for example, no...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using the default project for everything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>RBAC and sign-in \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Connect single sign-on (OpenID Connect) so people use company identities and MFA, and disable the shared admin account.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Shared admin login.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Secrets \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The repository must not hold plain secrets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Plain secrets in values files.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Multiple clusters \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Register a remote cluster with argocd cluster add, which creates a service account in that cluster and stores its token in Argo CD.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One Argo CD with unlimited reach into production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-secret-in-the-repository\">Incident: Secret in the repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A scan found a database password in a values file inside the configuration repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -rn &quot;password:&quot; apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml\ngit log --oneline -- apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;password:&quot;\u003c/span> apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">12:  password: &quot;Pr0d-Db-2024!&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline -- apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">9\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The password existed in nine commits. The team rotated it first, since history cannot be trusted to hide it, then replaced the value with a reference to the External Secrets Operator, added secret scanning in pull requests, and restricted access to the repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the default project for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared admin login\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Plain secrets in values files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One Argo CD with unlimited reach into production\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"projects","text":"Projects"},{"id":"rbac-and-sign-in","text":"RBAC and sign-in"},{"id":"secrets","text":"Secrets"},{"id":"multiple-clusters","text":"Multiple clusters"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-secret-in-the-repository","text":"Incident: Secret in the repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD security","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/security/"},{"t":"Projects","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/projects/"}],"text":"Argo CD is powerful: it can create anything in the clusters it manages, so protecting it matters. Three topics decide whether a shared installation is safe: who may do what (projects, RBAC and single sign-on), where secrets live, and how remote clusters are registered. What you will learn Use projects to limit sources, destinations and kinds Configure RBAC and single sign-on Handle secrets without storing them in plain text Register and secure multiple clusters Projects An AppProject limits which repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to, and which resource kinds it may create (for example, no cluster roles). Give each team a project. Do not run everything in default , which is unrestricted. apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: AppProject metadata: name: team-shop namespace: argocd spec: sourceRepos: [ \"https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\" ] destinations: - server: https://kubernetes.default.svc namespace: \"shop-*\" clusterResourceWhitelist: [] RBAC and sign-in Connect single sign-on (OpenID Connect) so people use company identities and MFA, and disable the shared admin account. Map groups to roles in argocd-rbac-cm : viewers see, developers sync their project, platform administrators manage projects. Use project roles and tokens for automation with limited scope. Secrets The repository must not hold plain secrets. Common approaches: sealed or encrypted secrets committed in encrypted form, External Secrets Operator that creates Kubernetes Secrets from a cloud secrets manager, or a plugin that injects values at render time. Choose one and standardise it. Remember that Argo CD itself stores cluster credentials and repository credentials as secrets, so protect its namespace. Multiple clusters Register a remote cluster with argocd cluster add , which creates a service account in that cluster and stores its token in Argo CD. Prefer a hub model with one control cluster and narrow roles per target, or one Argo CD per environment tier so a compromise of non-production cannot reach production. Limit what the remote service account can do where possible. Predict first. Teams share one Argo CD. How do you stop team A deploying into team B's namespaces? Your guess Answer: Give each team an AppProject with its own allowed destinations and sources, and bind roles to that project. Projects are the boundary. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Access, projects, secrets and multi-cluster fits together Flow in order: 1, Projects to RBAC and sign-in; 2, RBAC and sign-in to Secrets; 3, Secrets to Multiple clusters. Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Projects Step What it does: An AppProject limits which repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to, and which resource kinds it may create (for example, no... If it fails or is weak: Using the default project for everything. RBAC and sign-in Security What it does: Connect single sign-on (OpenID Connect) so people use company identities and MFA, and disable the shared admin account. If it fails or is weak: Shared admin login. Secrets Security What it does: The repository must not hold plain secrets. If it fails or is weak: Plain secrets in values files. Multiple clusters Step What it does: Register a remote cluster with argocd cluster add, which creates a service account in that cluster and stores its token in Argo CD. If it fails or is weak: One Argo CD with unlimited reach into production. Incident: Secret in the repository Incident A scan found a database password in a values file inside the configuration repository. $ grep -rn \"password:\" apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml 12: password: \"Pr0d-Db-2024!\" $ git log --oneline -- apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml | wc -"},"argocd/debugging-argo-cd":{"html":"\u003cp>Argo CD problems look like a handful of patterns: it cannot read the repository, it cannot render the manifests, it cannot apply them, or the applied resources are unhealthy. Walking those four stages in order finds almost every cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Diagnose repository and credential errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnose render failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnose apply and permission failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnose health problems and endless OutOfSync\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"four-stages\">Four stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fetch:\u003c/strong> can the repo server clone the repository and find the path and revision? 2. \u003cstrong>Render:\u003c/strong> does Helm or Kustomize produce valid manifests with the given values? 3. \u003cstrong>Apply:\u003c/strong> are the manifests accepted by the API server, and does the destination allow them? 4. \u003cstrong>Health:\u003c/strong> do the created resources become healthy? \u003ccode>argocd app get NAME\u003c/code> shows conditions for the first failing stage.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fetch-and-render\">Fetch and render\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Errors like &quot;authentication required&quot;, &quot;revision not found&quot; or &quot;app path does not exist&quot; are fetch problems: check repository credentials, branch names and the folder. Render errors show the Helm or Kustomize message (&quot;nil pointer evaluating&quot;, &quot;unable to find one &#39;resource&#39;&quot;): reproduce locally with \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>kustomize build\u003c/code> using the same values. Check that the repo server has network access to chart repositories.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apply-failures\">Apply failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;is forbidden&quot; means the Argo CD service account lacks permission; &quot;not permitted in project&quot; means the project does not allow that kind or destination; webhook or admission errors come from the cluster; &quot;immutable field&quot; needs the resource to be replaced; &quot;CRD not found&quot; means ordering: sync the CRD first using a wave. Use \u003ccode>argocd app sync NAME --dry-run\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>argocd app diff\u003c/code> to see what would be applied.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-and-outofsync-loops\">Health and OutOfSync loops\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Degraded application needs ordinary Kubernetes debugging on the resources: events, logs, probes. An application that is OutOfSync again right after a sync usually has another controller changing a field (add \u003ccode>ignoreDifferences\u003c/code>), a mutating webhook, or defaults added by the API server. Compare the live and desired YAML for the differing field.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"useful-commands\">Useful commands\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>argocd app get NAME\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>argocd app diff NAME\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>argocd app history NAME\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>argocd app logs NAME\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>kubectl -n argocd logs deploy/argocd-repo-server\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>kubectl -n argocd logs statefulset/argocd-application-controller\u003c/code>. To roll back, revert the commit; the \u003ccode>argocd app rollback\u003c/code> command is limited when automatic sync is on.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Sync fails with &quot;namespaces shop is forbidden&quot; in a project. What do you check?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Whether the project allows that destination namespace and whether the target service account has permission.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Project and RBAC.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Debugging Argo CD fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Four stages\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fetch and render\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Apply failures\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Health and OutOfSync loops\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Useful commands\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Four stages to Fetch and render; 2, Fetch and render to Apply failures; 3, Apply failures to Health and OutOfSync loops; 4, Health and OutOfSync loops to Useful commands.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Four stages \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Fetch: can the repo server clone the repository and find the path and revision?\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not reproducing the render locally.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Fetch and render \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Errors like &quot;authentication required&quot;, &quot;revision not found&quot; or &quot;app path does not exist&quot; are fetch problems: check repository credentials, branch names and the folder.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forcing syncs with replace without understanding the cause.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Apply failures \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> &quot;is forbidden&quot; means the Argo CD service account lacks permission; &quot;not permitted in project&quot; means the project does not allow that kind or destination; webhook or...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Health and OutOfSync loops \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A Degraded application needs ordinary Kubernetes debugging on the resources: events, logs, probes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing things before reading the condition.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Useful commands \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> argocd app get NAME, argocd app diff NAME, argocd app history NAME, argocd app logs NAME, kubectl -n argocd logs deploy/argocd-repo-server, and kubectl -n argocd logs...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-forever-outofsync\">Incident: Forever OutOfSync\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An application showed OutOfSync after every sync although nothing had changed in the repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app diff shop-api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app diff shop-api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">===== apps/Deployment shop/api ======\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  spec:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    replicas:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt;     2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&gt;     5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A horizontal autoscaler was setting replicas to 5 while the repository said 2. The team removed replicas from the manifest and added ignoreDifferences for that field, so each tool owned its own setting and the application stayed Synced.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Changing things before reading the condition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not reproducing the render locally\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fighting autoscalers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forcing syncs with replace without understanding the cause\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"four-stages","text":"Four stages"},{"id":"fetch-and-render","text":"Fetch and render"},{"id":"apply-failures","text":"Apply failures"},{"id":"health-and-outofsync-loops","text":"Health and OutOfSync loops"},{"id":"useful-commands","text":"Useful commands"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-forever-outofsync","text":"Incident: Forever OutOfSync"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"},{"t":"Automated sync policy","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/auto_sync/"}],"text":"Argo CD problems look like a handful of patterns: it cannot read the repository, it cannot render the manifests, it cannot apply them, or the applied resources are unhealthy. Walking those four stages in order finds almost every cause. What you will learn Diagnose repository and credential errors Diagnose render failures Diagnose apply and permission failures Diagnose health problems and endless OutOfSync Four stages Fetch: can the repo server clone the repository and find the path and revision? 2. Render: does Helm or Kustomize produce valid manifests with the given values? 3. Apply: are the manifests accepted by the API server, and does the destination allow them? 4. Health: do the created resources become healthy? argocd app get NAME shows conditions for the first failing stage. Fetch and render Errors like \"authentication required\", \"revision not found\" or \"app path does not exist\" are fetch problems: check repository credentials, branch names and the folder. Render errors show the Helm or Kustomize message (\"nil pointer evaluating\", \"unable to find one &#39;resource&#39;\"): reproduce locally with helm template or kustomize build using the same values. Check that the repo server has network access to chart repositories. Apply failures \"is forbidden\" means the Argo CD service account lacks permission; \"not permitted in project\" means the project does not allow that kind or destination; webhook or admission errors come from the cluster; \"immutable field\" needs the resource to be replaced; \"CRD not found\" means ordering: sync the CRD first using a wave. Use argocd app sync NAME --dry-run and argocd app diff to see what would be applied. Health and OutOfSync loops A Degraded application needs ordinary Kubernetes debugging on the resources: events, logs, probes. An application that is OutOfSync again right after a sync usually has another controller changing a field (add ignoreDifferences ), a mutating webhook, or defaults added by the API server. Compare the live and desired YAML for the differing field. Useful commands argocd app get NAME , argocd app diff NAME , argocd app history NAME , argocd app logs NAME , kubectl -n argocd logs deploy/argocd-repo-server , and kubectl -n argocd logs statefulset/argocd-application-controller . To roll back, revert the commit; the argocd app rollback command is limited when automatic sync is on. Predict first. Sync fails with \"namespaces shop is forbidden\" in a project. What do you check? Your guess Answer: Whether the project allows that destination namespace and whether the target service account has permission. Project and RBAC. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Debugging Argo CD fits together Flow in order: 1, Four stages to Fetch and render; 2, Fetch and render to Apply failures; 3, Apply failures to Health and OutOfSync loops; 4, Health and OutOfSync loops to Useful commands. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Four stages Step What it does: Fetch: can the repo server clone the repository and find the path and revision? If it fails or is weak: Not reproducing the render locally. Fetch and render Step What it does: Errors like \"authentication required\", \"revision not found\" or \"app path does not exist\" are fetch problems: check repository credentials, branch names and the folder. If it fails or is weak: Forcing syncs with replace without understanding the cause. Apply failures Step What it does: \"is forbidden\" means the Argo CD service account lacks permission; \"not permitted in project\" means the project does not allow that kind or destination; webhook or... If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Health and OutOfSync loops Step What it does: A Degraded application needs ord"},"argocd/argocd-sso-rbac-and-audit":{"html":"\u003cp>Argo CD starts with a shared admin account, which is fine for a first test and wrong for a team. Production use needs identity, roles and evidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>OIDC single sign-on and removing local admin\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing RBAC policy for roles and groups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Limiting scope with AppProjects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audit trails and monitoring\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sso\">SSO\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# argocd-cm\ndata:\n  url: https://argocd.example.com\n  oidc.config: |\n    name: Company SSO\n    issuer: https://login.example.com\n    clientID: argocd\n    clientSecret: $oidc.clientSecret\n    requestedScopes: [&quot;openid&quot;, &quot;profile&quot;, &quot;email&quot;, &quot;groups&quot;]\n  admin.enabled: &quot;false&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># argocd-cm\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">data:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">url:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://argocd.example.com\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">oidc.config:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\n    name: Company SSO\n    issuer: https://login.example.com\n    clientID: argocd\n    clientSecret: $oidc.clientSecret\n    requestedScopes: [&quot;openid&quot;, &quot;profile&quot;, &quot;email&quot;, &quot;groups&quot;]\n\u003c/span>  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">admin.enabled:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;false&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Store the client secret in the Argo CD secret, not in the config map. After SSO works, disable the local \u003ccode>admin\u003c/code> account.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rbac\">RBAC\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"csv\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# argocd-rbac-cm: policy.csv\np, role:dev, applications, get, shop/*, allow\np, role:dev, applications, sync, shop/*, allow\np, role:release, applications, override, shop/*, allow\ng, shop-developers, role:dev\ng, platform-oncall, role:admin\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\"># argocd-rbac-cm: policy.csv\np, role:dev, applications, get, shop/*, allow\np, role:dev, applications, sync, shop/*, allow\np, role:release, applications, override, shop/*, allow\ng, shop-developers, role:dev\ng, platform-oncall, role:admin\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Policies read as subject, resource, action, object. The object is usually \u003ccode>project/application\u003c/code>. Map identity provider groups to roles, and default to read-only for everyone else with \u003ccode>policy.default: role:readonly\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"projects\">Projects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>AppProject\u003c/strong> limits which Git repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to and which Kubernetes kinds it may create. This stops a team from deploying to another team&#39;s namespace or creating cluster-wide resources.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"spec:\n  sourceRepos: [&quot;https://git.example.com/shop/*&quot;]\n  destinations:\n    - {server: https://kubernetes.default.svc, namespace: shop-*}\n  clusterResourceWhitelist: []\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">sourceRepos:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://git.example.com/shop/*&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destinations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">server:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://kubernetes.default.svc\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-*\u003c/span>}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">clusterResourceWhitelist:\u003c/span> []\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"audit\">Audit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Argo CD emits events and API logs for logins, syncs and changes. Ship them to central logging and alert on admin-level actions and manual syncs of production. Git history shows what changed, and Argo logs show who triggered it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Argo CD: SSO, RBAC and audit fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">SSO\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">RBAC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Projects\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, SSO to RBAC; 2, RBAC to Projects; 3, Projects to Audit.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>SSO \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Store the client secret in the Argo CD secret, not in the config map.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Shared admin credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>RBAC \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Policies read as subject, resource, action, object.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Giving all developers sync rights in production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Projects \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An AppProject limits which Git repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to and which Kubernetes kinds it may create.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Projects that allow any repository and cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Audit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Argo CD emits events and API logs for logins, syncs and changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No central audit logs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-who-synced-production\">Incident: Who synced production?\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A bad sync happened and no one could say who triggered it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">application: shop-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">operation: sync initiated by admin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">revision: HEAD   health: Degraded\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Everyone used the shared admin login. The team enabled SSO, disabled admin, gave developers sync rights only on non-production projects and required changes to production to come through reviewed Git commits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Shared admin credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Projects that allow any repository and cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving all developers sync rights in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No central audit logs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"sso","text":"SSO"},{"id":"rbac","text":"RBAC"},{"id":"projects","text":"Projects"},{"id":"audit","text":"Audit"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-who-synced-production","text":"Incident: Who synced production?"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD user management","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/user-management/"},{"t":"Argo CD RBAC","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/rbac/"}],"text":"Argo CD starts with a shared admin account, which is fine for a first test and wrong for a team. Production use needs identity, roles and evidence. What you will learn OIDC single sign-on and removing local admin Writing RBAC policy for roles and groups Limiting scope with AppProjects Audit trails and monitoring SSO # argocd-cm data: url: https://argocd.example.com oidc.config: | name: Company SSO issuer: https://login.example.com clientID: argocd clientSecret: $oidc.clientSecret requestedScopes: [\"openid\", \"profile\", \"email\", \"groups\"] admin.enabled: \"false\" Store the client secret in the Argo CD secret, not in the config map. After SSO works, disable the local admin account. RBAC # argocd-rbac-cm: policy.csv p, role:dev, applications, get, shop/*, allow p, role:dev, applications, sync, shop/*, allow p, role:release, applications, override, shop/*, allow g, shop-developers, role:dev g, platform-oncall, role:admin Policies read as subject, resource, action, object. The object is usually project/application . Map identity provider groups to roles, and default to read-only for everyone else with policy.default: role:readonly . Projects An AppProject limits which Git repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to and which Kubernetes kinds it may create. This stops a team from deploying to another team&#39;s namespace or creating cluster-wide resources. spec: sourceRepos: [ \"https://git.example.com/shop/*\" ] destinations: - { server: https://kubernetes.default.svc , namespace: shop-* } clusterResourceWhitelist: [] Audit Argo CD emits events and API logs for logins, syncs and changes. Ship them to central logging and alert on admin-level actions and manual syncs of production. Git history shows what changed, and Argo logs show who triggered it. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Argo CD: SSO, RBAC and audit fits together Flow in order: 1, SSO to RBAC; 2, RBAC to Projects; 3, Projects to Audit. Step Security Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. SSO Step What it does: Store the client secret in the Argo CD secret, not in the config map. If it fails or is weak: Shared admin credentials. RBAC Security What it does: Policies read as subject, resource, action, object. If it fails or is weak: Giving all developers sync rights in production. Projects Step What it does: An AppProject limits which Git repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to and which Kubernetes kinds it may create. If it fails or is weak: Projects that allow any repository and cluster. Audit Observability What it does: Argo CD emits events and API logs for logins, syncs and changes. If it fails or is weak: No central audit logs. Incident: Who synced production? Incident A bad sync happened and no one could say who triggered it. application: shop-prod operation: sync initiated by admin revision: HEAD health: Degraded Everyone used the shared admin login. The team enabled SSO, disabled admin, gave developers sync rights only on non-production projects and required changes to production to come through reviewed Git commits. Common mistakes Shared admin credentials Projects that allow any repository and cluster Giving all developers sync rights in production No central audit logs"},"argocd/argocd-notifications-and-image-updates":{"html":"\u003cp>A deployment tool that fails silently is dangerous. And a team that edits version numbers by hand for every release wastes time and makes mistakes. Two small components address these.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Triggers, templates and subscriptions in notifications\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Routing alerts by environment and severity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automating image tag updates through Git\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping automation reviewable\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"notifications\">Notifications\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# argocd-notifications-cm (excerpt)\ndata:\n  service.slack: |\n    token: $slack-token\n  trigger.on-sync-failed: |\n    - when: app.status.operationState.phase in ['Error', 'Failed']\n      send: [app-sync-failed]\n  template.app-sync-failed: |\n    message: |\n      Sync of {{.app.metadata.name}} failed: {{.app.status.operationState.message}}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># argocd-notifications-cm (excerpt)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">data:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service.slack:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\n    token: $slack-token\n\u003c/span>  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">trigger.on-sync-failed:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\n    - when: app.status.operationState.phase in [&#x27;Error&#x27;, &#x27;Failed&#x27;]\n      send: [app-sync-failed]\n\u003c/span>  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template.app-sync-failed:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\n    message: |\n      Sync of {{.app.metadata.name}} failed: {{.app.status.operationState.message}}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# on an Application\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.on-sync-failed.slack: shop-deploys\n    notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.on-health-degraded.slack: shop-deploys\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># on an Application\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">annotations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.on-sync-failed.slack:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-deploys\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.on-health-degraded.slack:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-deploys\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>trigger\u003c/strong> decides when, a \u003cstrong>template\u003c/strong> formats the message and a \u003cstrong>subscription\u003c/strong> says who receives it. Send routine events to a channel, and page people only for production health problems, to avoid alert fatigue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"image-updates\">Image updates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>Argo CD Image Updater\u003c/strong> or a pipeline step watches a registry and records a new tag in Git, either by committing to the manifest repository or opening a pull request.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"metadata:\n  annotations:\n    argocd-image-updater.argoproj.io/image-list: web=registry.example.com/shop/web\n    argocd-image-updater.argoproj.io/web.update-strategy: semver\n    argocd-image-updater.argoproj.io/write-back-method: git\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">annotations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">argocd-image-updater.argoproj.io/image-list:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web=registry.example.com/shop/web\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">argocd-image-updater.argoproj.io/web.update-strategy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">semver\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">argocd-image-updater.argoproj.io/write-back-method:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">git\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use a version strategy that fits your release process. For production, prefer pull requests with approval, and use digests when you need an exact immutable reference.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-git-as-truth\">Keep Git as truth\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Automatic updates should land as commits so history, review and rollback still work. Never let a tool edit live objects while Git says something else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Argo CD: notifications and image updates fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Notifications\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Image updates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Keep Git as truth\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Notifications to Image updates; 2, Image updates to Keep Git as truth.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Notifications \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A trigger decides when, a template formats the message and a subscription says who receives it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No notifications for failed syncs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Image updates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The Argo CD Image Updater or a pipeline step watches a registry and records a new tag in Git, either by committing to the manifest repository or opening a pull request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Image updaters that bypass Git.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Keep Git as truth \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Automatic updates should land as commits so history, review and rollback still work.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never let a tool edit live objects while Git says something else.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-silent-friday-failure\">Incident: The silent Friday failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A failed sync was noticed three days later.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">phase: Failed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">message: ComparisonError: failed to generate manifest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">notifications subscribed: none\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Production deployments failed over the weekend and customers kept seeing the old version. The team added failure notifications to a team channel, paging for production degraded health, and a dashboard of apps out of sync for over an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No notifications for failed syncs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Paging for every event\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Image updaters that bypass Git\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using moving tags like latest\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"notifications","text":"Notifications"},{"id":"image-updates","text":"Image updates"},{"id":"keep-git-as-truth","text":"Keep Git as truth"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-silent-friday-failure","text":"Incident: The silent Friday failure"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD notifications","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/notifications/"},{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"A deployment tool that fails silently is dangerous. And a team that edits version numbers by hand for every release wastes time and makes mistakes. Two small components address these. What you will learn Triggers, templates and subscriptions in notifications Routing alerts by environment and severity Automating image tag updates through Git Keeping automation reviewable Notifications # argocd-notifications-cm (excerpt) data: service.slack: | token: $slack-token trigger.on-sync-failed: | - when: app.status.operationState.phase in [&#x27;Error&#x27;, &#x27;Failed&#x27;] send: [app-sync-failed] template.app-sync-failed: | message: | Sync of {{.app.metadata.name}} failed: {{.app.status.operationState.message}} # on an Application metadata: annotations: notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.on-sync-failed.slack: shop-deploys notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.on-health-degraded.slack: shop-deploys A trigger decides when, a template formats the message and a subscription says who receives it. Send routine events to a channel, and page people only for production health problems, to avoid alert fatigue. Image updates The Argo CD Image Updater or a pipeline step watches a registry and records a new tag in Git, either by committing to the manifest repository or opening a pull request. metadata: annotations: argocd-image-updater.argoproj.io/image-list: web=registry.example.com/shop/web argocd-image-updater.argoproj.io/web.update-strategy: semver argocd-image-updater.argoproj.io/write-back-method: git Use a version strategy that fits your release process. For production, prefer pull requests with approval, and use digests when you need an exact immutable reference. Keep Git as truth Automatic updates should land as commits so history, review and rollback still work. Never let a tool edit live objects while Git says something else. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Argo CD: notifications and image updates fits together Flow in order: 1, Notifications to Image updates; 2, Image updates to Keep Git as truth. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Notifications Step What it does: A trigger decides when, a template formats the message and a subscription says who receives it. If it fails or is weak: No notifications for failed syncs. Image updates Step What it does: The Argo CD Image Updater or a pipeline step watches a registry and records a new tag in Git, either by committing to the manifest repository or opening a pull request. If it fails or is weak: Image updaters that bypass Git. Keep Git as truth Step What it does: Automatic updates should land as commits so history, review and rollback still work. If it fails or is weak: Never let a tool edit live objects while Git says something else. Incident: The silent Friday failure Incident A failed sync was noticed three days later. phase: Failed message: ComparisonError: failed to generate manifest notifications subscribed: none Production deployments failed over the weekend and customers kept seeing the old version. The team added failure notifications to a team channel, paging for production degraded health, and a dashboard of apps out of sync for over an hour. Common mistakes No notifications for failed syncs Paging for every event Image updaters that bypass Git Using moving tags like latest"},"argocd/progressive-delivery-with-argo-rollouts":{"html":"\u003cp>A rolling update checks readiness, not user experience. Progressive delivery adds real traffic shifting and metric analysis, so you can release often with less fear.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Canary and blue-green strategies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Defining steps, weights and pauses\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automated analysis against metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Aborting and promoting\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-canary-rollout\">A canary rollout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Rollout\nmetadata:\n  name: web\nspec:\n  replicas: 6\n  selector:\n    matchLabels: {app: web}\n  strategy:\n    canary:\n      steps:\n        - setWeight: 10\n        - pause: {duration: 5m}\n        - analysis:\n            templates:\n              - templateName: error-rate\n        - setWeight: 50\n        - pause: {duration: 10m}\n        - setWeight: 100\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels: {app: web}\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: web\n          image: registry.example.com/shop/web:1.9.0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Rollout\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">strategy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">canary:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">setWeight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pause:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">duration:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">5m\u003c/span>}\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">analysis:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">templates:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">templateName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">error-rate\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">setWeight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pause:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">duration:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">10m\u003c/span>}\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">setWeight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>}\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/web:1.9.0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The canary receives a small share, then more if checks pass. A traffic router such as an ingress controller or mesh gives accurate percentages, otherwise weights follow pod counts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"analysis\">Analysis\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: AnalysisTemplate\nmetadata:\n  name: error-rate\nspec:\n  metrics:\n    - name: error-rate\n      interval: 1m\n      count: 5\n      failureLimit: 1\n      successCondition: result[0] &lt; 0.02\n      provider:\n        prometheus:\n          address: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\n          query: sum(rate(http_requests_total{app=&quot;web&quot;,code=~&quot;5..&quot;}[2m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total{app=&quot;web&quot;}[2m]))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AnalysisTemplate\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">error-rate\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metrics:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">error-rate\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">interval:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">1m\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">count:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">failureLimit:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">successCondition:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">result[0]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&lt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.02\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">provider:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prometheus:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">address:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">query:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sum(rate(http_requests_total{app=&quot;web&quot;,code=~&quot;5..&quot;}[2m]))\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sum(rate(http_requests_total{app=&quot;web&quot;}[2m]))\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the metric breaches the condition, the rollout \u003cstrong>aborts\u003c/strong> and traffic returns to the stable version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operate\">Operate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl argo rollouts get rollout web --watch\nkubectl argo rollouts promote web\nkubectl argo rollouts abort web\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl argo rollouts get rollout web --watch\nkubectl argo rollouts promote web\nkubectl argo rollouts abort web\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Blue-green\u003c/strong> keeps the full old version running and switches all traffic at once after checks, which uses double resources but allows instant rollback. Use Argo CD to sync the Rollout from Git, and keep database changes backward compatible, because traffic may run both versions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Progressive delivery with Argo Rollouts fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A canary rollout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Analysis\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Operate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, A canary rollout to Analysis; 2, Analysis to Operate.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>A canary rollout \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The canary receives a small share, then more if checks pass.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Weights that follow pod counts without a traffic router.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Analysis \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> If the metric breaches the condition, the rollout aborts and traffic returns to the stable version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Canary with no automated analysis.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Operate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Blue-green keeps the full old version running and switches all traffic at once after checks, which uses double resources but allows instant rollback.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Database changes that break the old version.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-canary-that-stopped-itself\">Incident: The canary that stopped itself\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A release with a bug was aborted automatically at 10 percent.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">step 3/6 analysis: error-rate = 0.061\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">successCondition result[0] &lt; 0.02 failed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Rollout aborted: canary scaled to 0, stable at 100%\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A change introduced a bug that affected one payment path. The analysis caught the error rate within five minutes, and only a tenth of traffic was affected. The team fixed the bug, added a test and kept the same analysis for every release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Canary with no automated analysis\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Weights that follow pod counts without a traffic router\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Database changes that break the old version\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pause durations too short to see problems\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-canary-rollout","text":"A canary rollout"},{"id":"analysis","text":"Analysis"},{"id":"operate","text":"Operate"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-canary-that-stopped-itself","text":"Incident: The canary that stopped itself"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo Rollouts documentation","u":"https://argo-rollouts.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"},{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"A rolling update checks readiness, not user experience. Progressive delivery adds real traffic shifting and metric analysis, so you can release often with less fear. What you will learn Canary and blue-green strategies Defining steps, weights and pauses Automated analysis against metrics Aborting and promoting A canary rollout apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Rollout metadata: name: web spec: replicas: 6 selector: matchLabels: { app: web } strategy: canary: steps: - setWeight: 10 - pause: { duration: 5m } - analysis: templates: - templateName: error-rate - setWeight: 50 - pause: { duration: 10m } - setWeight: 100 template: metadata: labels: { app: web } spec: containers: - name: web image: registry.example.com/shop/web:1.9.0 The canary receives a small share, then more if checks pass. A traffic router such as an ingress controller or mesh gives accurate percentages, otherwise weights follow pod counts. Analysis apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: AnalysisTemplate metadata: name: error-rate spec: metrics: - name: error-rate interval: 1m count: 5 failureLimit: 1 successCondition: result[0] \u003c 0.02 provider: prometheus: address: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090 query: sum(rate(http_requests_total{app=\"web\",code=~\"5..\"}[2m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total{app=\"web\"}[2m])) If the metric breaches the condition, the rollout aborts and traffic returns to the stable version. Operate kubectl argo rollouts get rollout web --watch kubectl argo rollouts promote web kubectl argo rollouts abort web Blue-green keeps the full old version running and switches all traffic at once after checks, which uses double resources but allows instant rollback. Use Argo CD to sync the Rollout from Git, and keep database changes backward compatible, because traffic may run both versions. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Progressive delivery with Argo Rollouts fits together Flow in order: 1, A canary rollout to Analysis; 2, Analysis to Operate. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. A canary rollout Step What it does: The canary receives a small share, then more if checks pass. If it fails or is weak: Weights that follow pod counts without a traffic router. Analysis Step What it does: If the metric breaches the condition, the rollout aborts and traffic returns to the stable version. If it fails or is weak: Canary with no automated analysis. Operate Step What it does: Blue-green keeps the full old version running and switches all traffic at once after checks, which uses double resources but allows instant rollback. If it fails or is weak: Database changes that break the old version. Incident: The canary that stopped itself Incident A release with a bug was aborted automatically at 10 percent. step 3/6 analysis: error-rate = 0.061 successCondition result[0] \u003c 0.02 failed Rollout aborted: canary scaled to 0, stable at 100% A change introduced a bug that affected one payment path. The analysis caught the error rate within five minutes, and only a tenth of traffic was affected. The team fixed the bug, added a test and kept the same analysis for every release. Common mistakes Canary with no automated analysis Weights that follow pod counts without a traffic router Database changes that break the old version Pause durations too short to see problems"},"argocd/argocd-disaster-recovery-and-upgrades":{"html":"\u003cp>A Git-driven delivery tool has a pleasant recovery story, because applications are described in a repository. It is not automatic, though: you still need to protect Argo CD&#39;s own settings, secrets and cluster credentials, and upgrade it carefully.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What is in Git and what is not\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backing up Argo CD configuration and secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding from a bootstrap application\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading safely\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-protect\">What to protect\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Application manifests live in Git. Not in Git: cluster credentials, repository credentials, SSO client secrets, TLS certificates and any application or project objects created by hand. Put the rest of the configuration in Git too, and keep the secrets in a secret manager.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backup\">Backup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd admin export -n argocd &gt; argocd-backup-$(date +%F).yaml\nkubectl get secret -n argocd -l argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type=cluster -o yaml &gt; clusters.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">argocd admin \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> -n argocd &gt; argocd-backup-$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span> +%F).yaml\nkubectl get secret -n argocd -l argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type=cluster -o yaml &gt; clusters.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Encrypt backup files and store them off-cluster. Test that the export can be imported into a fresh instance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rebuild\">Rebuild\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create namespace argocd\nkubectl apply -n argocd -f install.yaml\nkubectl apply -f bootstrap/root-app.yaml     # app of apps that creates everything else\nargocd app list\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create namespace argocd\nkubectl apply -n argocd -f install.yaml\nkubectl apply -f bootstrap/root-app.yaml     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># app of apps that creates everything else\u003c/span>\nargocd app list\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With the \u003cstrong>app-of-apps\u003c/strong> pattern, one root application points to a directory of other applications, so applying that single object restores the whole fleet. Application data in volumes is not in Git and needs its own backups.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrade\">Upgrade\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Read release notes for breaking changes and CRD updates, since Helm does not always upgrade CRDs. Upgrade one minor version at a time, first in a staging instance with a copy of production configuration. After the upgrade, check applications for new diffs or health changes, and keep the previous manifests to roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd version\nkubectl get applications -n argocd -o wide | grep -v Synced\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">argocd version\nkubectl get applications -n argocd -o wide | grep -v Synced\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Argo CD: disaster recovery and upgrades fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What to protect\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rebuild\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Upgrade\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, What to protect to Backup; 2, Backup to Rebuild; 3, Rebuild to Upgrade.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>What to protect \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Application manifests live in Git. Not in Git: cluster credentials, repository credentials, SSO client secrets, TLS certificates and any application or project objects...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Credentials that exist only in the live cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Backup \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Encrypt backup files and store them off-cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Rebuild \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> With the app-of-apps pattern, one root application points to a directory of other applications, so applying that single object restores the whole fleet.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never rehearsing a rebuild.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Upgrade \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Read release notes for breaking changes and CRD updates, since Helm does not always upgrade CRDs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping minor versions in upgrades.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-rebuilt-cluster-with-no-repository-access\">Incident: A rebuilt cluster with no repository access\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Argo CD came back but could not read private repositories.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ComparisonError: authentication required\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">rpc error: repository not found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">31 applications in Unknown state\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The Git credentials had been added through the UI and were not stored anywhere else. After rebuilding, Argo CD had no access to private repositories. The team moved credentials into a secret manager and declarative secrets, and rehearsed a full rebuild twice a year.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Credentials that exist only in the live cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not backing up Argo CD secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping minor versions in upgrades\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never rehearsing a rebuild\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-protect","text":"What to protect"},{"id":"backup","text":"Backup"},{"id":"rebuild","text":"Rebuild"},{"id":"upgrade","text":"Upgrade"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-a-rebuilt-cluster-with-no-repository-access","text":"Incident: A rebuilt cluster with no repository access"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD disaster recovery","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/disaster_recovery/"},{"t":"Argo CD upgrading","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/upgrading/overview/"}],"text":"A Git-driven delivery tool has a pleasant recovery story, because applications are described in a repository. It is not automatic, though: you still need to protect Argo CD&#39;s own settings, secrets and cluster credentials, and upgrade it carefully. What you will learn What is in Git and what is not Backing up Argo CD configuration and secrets Rebuilding from a bootstrap application Upgrading safely What to protect Application manifests live in Git. Not in Git: cluster credentials, repository credentials, SSO client secrets, TLS certificates and any application or project objects created by hand. Put the rest of the configuration in Git too, and keep the secrets in a secret manager. Backup argocd admin export -n argocd > argocd-backup-$( date +%F).yaml kubectl get secret -n argocd -l argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type=cluster -o yaml > clusters.yaml Encrypt backup files and store them off-cluster. Test that the export can be imported into a fresh instance. Rebuild kubectl create namespace argocd kubectl apply -n argocd -f install.yaml kubectl apply -f bootstrap/root-app.yaml # app of apps that creates everything else argocd app list With the app-of-apps pattern, one root application points to a directory of other applications, so applying that single object restores the whole fleet. Application data in volumes is not in Git and needs its own backups. Upgrade Read release notes for breaking changes and CRD updates, since Helm does not always upgrade CRDs. Upgrade one minor version at a time, first in a staging instance with a copy of production configuration. After the upgrade, check applications for new diffs or health changes, and keep the previous manifests to roll back. argocd version kubectl get applications -n argocd -o wide | grep -v Synced How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Argo CD: disaster recovery and upgrades fits together Flow in order: 1, What to protect to Backup; 2, Backup to Rebuild; 3, Rebuild to Upgrade. Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. What to protect Step What it does: Application manifests live in Git. Not in Git: cluster credentials, repository credentials, SSO client secrets, TLS certificates and any application or project objects... If it fails or is weak: Credentials that exist only in the live cluster. Backup Data store What it does: Encrypt backup files and store them off-cluster. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Rebuild Step What it does: With the app-of-apps pattern, one root application points to a directory of other applications, so applying that single object restores the whole fleet. If it fails or is weak: Never rehearsing a rebuild. Upgrade Step What it does: Read release notes for breaking changes and CRD updates, since Helm does not always upgrade CRDs. If it fails or is weak: Skipping minor versions in upgrades. Incident: A rebuilt cluster with no repository access Incident Argo CD came back but could not read private repositories. ComparisonError: authentication required rpc error: repository not found 31 applications in Unknown state The Git credentials had been added through the UI and were not stored anywhere else. After rebuilding, Argo CD had no access to private repositories. The team moved credentials into a secret manager and declarative secrets, and rehearsed a full rebuild twice a year. Common mistakes Credentials that exist only in the live cluster Not backing up Argo CD secrets Skipping minor versions in upgrades Never rehearsing a rebuild"}});